    body: "You don't need a separate app to edit photos on your iPhone. The built-in Photos app includes a full set of editing tools. You can adjust brightness, contrast, color, crop, straighten, add filters, and even remove red-eye — all without leaving the app you already use to view your pictures.\n\nThe best part: every edit is non-destructive. That means your original photo is always saved. If you make changes and later don't like them, you can tap \"Revert\" to instantly go back to the original. There's no risk of permanently ruining a photo.\n\nHere are the main editing tools available in the iPhone Photos app:\n\n**Auto Enhance** — A single tap that lets Apple's software automatically improve brightness, contrast, and color. Look for the wand icon (magic wand). It's a great starting point.\n\n**Adjust panel** — This is the main editing area with sliders for Exposure (overall brightness), Brilliance (recover detail in bright and dark areas), Highlights (brighten or darken the lightest parts), Shadows (lighten or darken dark areas), Contrast, Brightness, Black Point, Saturation, Vibrance, Warmth, Tint, Sharpness, and Noise Reduction. Each has a slider you drag left or right.\n\n**Filters** — Tap the three overlapping circles icon to see a row of artistic filters like Vivid, Dramatic, Noir, Fade, and more. Tap one to preview it, and use the slider at the bottom to control its intensity.\n\n**Crop and Rotate** — Tap the crop icon (a square with lines at the corners) to enter crop mode. Drag the corners to frame your shot differently. Tap the rotate icon to turn the photo 90 degrees. Drag the dial at the bottom to straighten a crooked horizon.\n\nTo access all of this: open a photo, tap \"Edit\" in the top right corner, make your changes, and tap \"Done\" to save. If you change your mind, tap \"Revert\" at any time to go back to the original.",
