    body: "After a long trip, your phone usually holds a few hundred photos all mixed together with grocery store pictures, screenshots, and shots of the dog. Finding the photos from a single trip can feel like searching for a needle in a haystack. Google Photos solves this problem with two free tools: smart suggestions that automatically build a trip album, and manual albums you can build in a couple of minutes. Both work on iPhone and Android, as long as you have the Google Photos app installed and signed in.\n\nThe first tool is Memories. Google Photos quietly looks at the date and location of every photo. When it sees a cluster of photos taken in a new city over several days, it suggests a trip album with a title like \"Trip to Paris\" or \"Weekend in San Diego.\" These suggestions show up at the top of the Photos screen and in the Memories tab. One tap accepts the suggestion and the trip album is built. Google even picks the best photos to feature on the album cover.\n\nThe second tool is the manual album. If Google missed a trip, or if you want to combine two short visits into one album, you can build it yourself. Open the Library tab at the bottom right, tap Albums, then tap New Album. Type a name like \"Yellowstone 2026.\" Tap Add Photos, scroll through your library, and tap each photo you want in the album. Google sorts them by date inside the album, so the trip plays back in the order it happened.\n\nA powerful trick is search. In the search bar at the top of Google Photos, type the name of a place, like \"Rome\" or \"Yellowstone.\" Google uses the location data baked into each photo to find every picture taken there, even years apart. Type \"beach\" and you see every beach photo in your library. Type a year and a month, like \"July 2025,\" and you see every photo from that month. Search alone can save you twenty minutes of scrolling.\n\nOnce a trip album is built, you can share it with family. Tap the share icon at the top of the album, then pick people from your contacts or copy a link. Anyone who opens the link can see the photos and add their own. This turns a family vacation into a shared album where each person's pictures land in one place. Years from now, that album is the family's record of the trip, neatly tucked away in Google Photos and ready to enjoy.",
