    body: "Everyone has done it. You finish typing a text, tap send, and a half-second later you see the typo, or worse, you realize the message went to the wrong person in your contacts. For decades, there was no way to take that back. Once a text was sent, it was gone. Apple changed this in iOS 16, which came out in late 2022. If both you and the person you texted are using an iPhone with iOS 17 or newer (iOS 18 is the current 2026 release, so this covers almost everyone), you have two full minutes to unsend a message after you tap send. You can also edit a typo in a sent message for fifteen minutes after sending. This feature has saved a lot of marriages and avoided a lot of awkward family group chat moments.\n\nThe feature only works for iMessages, which are the texts that show up in blue bubbles. It does not work for green bubble texts, which are regular SMS messages sent to Android phones. The reason is that iMessage runs through Apple's servers, so Apple can pull the message back before it shows up. Regular SMS goes through your phone carrier, and once the carrier delivers it, there is no way to take it back. If you see green bubbles in your conversation, the unsend button will not appear.\n\nWhen you unsend a message, the recipient sees a small note that says \"You unsent a message.\" They cannot see what the original message said. They only know that you sent something and then took it back. This is a small price to pay for being able to fix mistakes. The same is true for edits: when you edit a message, the recipient sees the corrected version with a small \"Edited\" tag. And they can tap that tag to see the original if they are curious. So an edit is more visible than an unsend, but neither one is invisible.\n\nThe two-minute window is strict. After two minutes pass, the unsend option disappears and the message stays in place. The fifteen-minute edit window is also strict. The lesson is to act fast. The moment you see your typo or the wrong recipient, do not panic, but do move quickly. Open the Messages app, find the message, and follow the steps below. With a little practice, you will fix your mistakes before the other person even has time to read the original.",
